{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792701485,"nonce":"5WnA8dn2s8571YdhztpXH8cVMq0=","title":"Bound LSP heap retention independently of persisted BLADE indexes","message":"User observed Rayzor LSP reaching roughly 1.1 GiB during RayDE stress tests. A live vmmap sample of the current server (PID 13431, 2026-10-22 21:33 local) has a 277.9 MiB physical footprint, peak 328.3 MiB, 210.2 MiB allocated across 1,577,621 malloc allocations, and 63.6 MiB fragmentation. This does not reproduce or disprove the larger reported peak. Evidence: /tmp/rayzor-lsp-active-memory-summary.txt.\n\nRetention audit:\n- LspContext retains two CompilationUnits by count, not bytes. Changed generations stay in sessions for undo, and allocating the next graph can temporarily leave three large graphs alive. Closing a page drops analyses but not sessions. Persisted BLADE editor indexes do not release their graphs.\n- Each graph retains parsed HaxeFile bodies, compiled TAST, static signature declarations, source maps, macro-only source ASTs, source overrides and macro state. Source and declaration data are duplicated across these owners.\n- EditorCache budgets complete snapshots using encoded JSON bytes rather than live serde_json::Value heap size. Numeric semantic-token arrays and object-heavy responses can greatly exceed that budget in RAM. Partial snapshots deep-clone response trees repeatedly.\n- WorkspaceIndex has an unbounded producer channel and retains declarations/docs for the whole workspace. A busy typing worker can leave the scanner queue growing.\n- Open buffer text is duplicated in frontend documents, worker open_files, compilation source_overrides, dirty-input snapshots and source/AST maps.\n- Macro replay keeps before/after static maps per file, plus shared statics and callback closures. Selective closure capture is already implemented, but object/closure cycles require separate verification.\n- Freed small allocations can remain in malloc arenas; vmmap reports 24 percent fragmentation in this sample. Reserved stacks are not the principal resident cost: 524 MiB of stack address space had under 1 MiB written/swapped. Loaded library mappings are mostly clean/reclaimable pages.\n\nActions: first evict obsolete generations before allocating replacements and retain their file-backed editor responses; measure actual in-memory snapshot cost or retain compact encoded responses; bound scanner queue; share immutable source text; trim replay-only macro state after completed analysis where later expansion semantics permit; use allocator pressure relief only after live owners have been released. Longer-term, persisted typed dependency modules are required to drop the last compiler graph without making a never-opened dependency tab cold again.","files":null}]}